```
The assertions in `PUT_ContainsContract` contract method are used to check that the return value of `Contains` indicates that the value `x` is in the list or not. If `Contains` correctly indicates that `x` is in the list, it should return `true` and `x` should have a location on the list (index `>= -1`). If `Contains` correctly indicates that `x` is not in the list, it should return `false` and `x` should not have a location on the list (index `= -1`).

In the given test, it is assumed that there is a variable precondition for testing i.e. the count, index of and last index of `x` as given above. 

`IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` methods are returning `-1` when the element is not found in the ArrayList and when `x` is not in the list. However, the contract test checks that `x`’s position is greater than or equal to `-1` (which is true when `x` is in the list) and is not equal to `-1` (which is true when `x` is not in the list).

It is important that you understand how the contract tests work and how they can help you catch issues with your code. The Pex tool and C# assertions enable you to write such tests and are useful for testing your code against a variety of different inputs.

Remember that a contract test asserts that the code is following your expectations, not the expectations of your code. In this example, the contract test checks for whether `Contains` is returning true or false, and tests how those boolean values affect the index of `x`.--------------------            if (!New_Ret) Assert.True(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1);
        }
    }
